  </div>
  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-builder data-answer="she said she would call me the next day" data-hint="Use said before the clause, change original you to reporter Alex’s me, and rebuild tomorrow as the next day." data-fix="She said she would call me the next day." data-success-message="Correct. Would reports the original future, me keeps Alex as listener, and the next day preserves the original tomorrow.">
    <h3>Build 2: keep person and time reference clear</h3><p class="tile-target">Alex reports Mina’s Monday words, “I’ll call you tomorrow.”</p>
    <div class="tile-bank" data-build-bank><button class="tile" data-build-tile="she">She</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="next">next</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="me">me</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="day">day</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="would">would</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="said">said</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="the">the</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="call">call</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="she">she</button></div>
    <div class="build-area" data-build-area data-placeholder="Tap words in order"></div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-build>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-build>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice and repair the reporting viewpoint</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="said" data-hint="The listener me follows the reporting verb directly, so use told." data-fix="Mina told me that she was late." data-why="Tell takes a listener directly; say would require Mina said that she was late or Mina said to me that she was late." data-success-message="Correct. Told me preserves the named listener in the required pattern.">
    <h3>Repair 1: choose the listener frame</h3><p>Tap the reporting verb that cannot take <span class="ex">me</span> directly here.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="mina">Mina</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="said">said</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="me">me</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="late">that she was late.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="my" data-hint="The folder belongs to original speaker Mina, not reporter Alex." data-fix="Mina said she would bring her folder." data-why="When Alex reports Mina’s words, Mina is she and her possessions use her." data-success-message="Correct. Her keeps the folder connected to original speaker Mina.">
    <h3>Repair 2: preserve ownership</h3><p>Alex is reporting Mina’s words, “I will bring my folder.” Tap the possessive that points to the wrong person.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="mina">Mina said</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="she">she would bring</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="my">my</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="folder">folder.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="yesterday" data-hint="Mina’s original yesterday is Sunday; Alex’s Tuesday yesterday would mean Monday." data-fix="Mina said she had sent the forms the day before." data-why="The day before preserves the day preceding Mina’s original Monday message." data-success-message="Correct. The day before keeps the original Sunday reference after the report moves to Tuesday.">
    <h3>Repair 3: preserve the original day</h3><p>Mina spoke Monday: “I sent the forms yesterday.” Alex reports Tuesday. Tap the time word that now points to the wrong day.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="mina">Mina said</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="sent">she had sent</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="forms">the forms</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="yesterday">yesterday.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Use it without exposing private messages</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to report a real private conversation, employer message, client statement, family discussion, medical detail, legal issue, travel problem, password, address, schedule, conflict, promise, or opinion. Use Mina, Alex, Jordan, Harbor Station, and Riverside Office. Real details are optional.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Original situation:</b> Mina speaks to Alex on Monday at Harbor Station. Create a four-part fictional statement with a current situation, completed event, ability, and future intention.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>New situation:</b> Alex reports later from Riverside Office to Jordan. State the reporting time and place before rebuilding pronouns and references.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Viewpoint control:</b> Use one common backshift, one explicit earlier Past Perfect layer, one modal shift, and one justified still-current form.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Accuracy:</b> Use <span class="ex">said</span> neutrally, <span class="ex">told + listener</span> once, and do not invent an exact quote, promise, or doubt.</div>

  <h2>Final production and check</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Final production: relay a 75–90 second fictional message</h3>
    <p>Use the supplied people and places. First state who spoke to whom, when, and where. Report a current situation, a completed earlier event, an ability, a future intention, and one still-current general fact. Use both <span class="ex">said</span> and <span class="ex">told + listener</span>. Rebuild every pronoun, possessive, day, and place reference. End by explaining one backshift and one deliberate no-backshift choice.</p>
    <p><b>Evidence check:</b> Can the listener identify every person behind each pronoun, recover the original time and place, distinguish earlier event from later report, hear whether <span class="ex">’d</span> means <span class="ex">would</span> or <span class="ex">had</span>, and tell which details came from the original speaker rather than the reporter?</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You preserved listener pattern, explicit earlier time, and still-current framing instead of changing every form mechanically.">
    <h3>Final check: defend the reported viewpoint</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Mina told Alex that she was running late." data-hint="The sentence names listener Alex directly, so tell must precede him." data-fix="Mina told Alex that she was running late."><p class="quiz-question">1. Which sentence uses the named-listener pattern accurately?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Mina told Alex that she was running late.">Mina told Alex that she was running late.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Mina said Alex that she was running late.">Mina said Alex that she was running late.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Mina told that she was running late.">Mina told that she was running late.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Mina said her train had been canceled that morning." data-hint="Use her for Mina’s train, Past Perfect for the explicitly earlier event, and that morning for the original Monday morning." data-fix="Mina said her train had been canceled that morning."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which careful later report preserves person and earlier-event time?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Mina said her train had been canceled that morning.">Mina said her train had been canceled that morning.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Mina said my train has canceled tomorrow.">Mina said my train has canceled tomorrow.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Mina told her train was cancel this morning.">Mina told her train was cancel this morning.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The guide said the museum closes at six." data-hint="The prompt requests the still-current schedule presented as useful now." data-fix="The guide said the museum closes at six."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which report deliberately keeps a currently valid schedule in the present?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The guide said the museum closes at six.">The guide said the museum closes at six.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The guide said the museum closing at six yesterday tomorrow.">The guide said the museum closing at six yesterday tomorrow.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The guide told that the museum close at six.">The guide told that the museum close at six.</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Reflect: whose viewpoint does each word serve?</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Explain the decision path</h3>
    <p>Complete these ideas aloud: <span class="ex">Said is followed by...</span> <span class="ex">Told is followed by...</span> <span class="ex">I change a pronoun when...</span> <span class="ex">I change a time word when...</span> <span class="ex">Backshift is common when...</span> <span class="ex">I may keep a present form when...</span></p>
    <p>Create a direct quotation and a responsible paraphrase of one fictional message. Explain why only the exact version uses quotation marks and whether the paraphrase adds any stance.</p>
